  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will continue to sink southward\nthrough the area this afternoon and early evening. Showers and\nstorms continue to blossom near the Albemarle and Pamlico Sounds\nthis afternoon. West to northwest low-level flow should tend to\nlimit the strength of convergence along the front. However, as \nthe front encounters the developing seabreeze, there should be a\nlocal enhancement to low-level forcing, especially along and \nsouth of HWY 70. This is the area where the best chance for \nsustained, deep convection exists. The risk is conditional on \nsufficient low- level forcing, but should this occur, a short- \nlived risk of strong to marginally severe thunderstorms may \ndevelop thanks to elevated deep layer shear (~35kt) and moderate\ninstability (MLCAPE up to 1500J/kg). Machine learning guidance \nisn't all that excited about this potential, and this likely \nspeaks to the conditionality of the threat. This risk will \nquickly end from north to south as the front shifts off the \nCrystal Coast by sunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continue to trend stronger with an \nupper level ridge that will be expanding across the Southern \nU.S. this week. In fact, ensemble guidance is now forecasting \n500mb heights around 600dm with this ridge, the strength of \nwhich is very unusual for this time of year. Normally, this \nwould spell anomalous heat for ENC. However, a pocket of \n\"cooler\" thicknesses is forecast to be over the Carolinas \nassociated with the post-frontal northeasterly flow regime, \nespecially Tuesday and Wednesday. While it will be mostly dry, \nthose cooler thicknesses may shield ENC from the anomalous heat \nthat other parts of the South will experience. That pocket of \nlower thicknesses may begin to erode by the end of the week, \nwith temperatures warming by then. However, guidance differ on \nwhen/if this will occur.",
